// cin.ignore() does removes leftover newline (\n) after cin >> . What it means :- 
Suppose the input is:
A
hello world 

and our code is:

char c;
string s;
cin >> c;
getline(cin, s);

cin >> c; reads: A and stores: c = 'A'; 
But when you press Enter while inputting the string (cin takes input from the user, so 1st input will be a character c, then we click enter and then 2nd input of string is given), a newline character (\n) is also entered. 
So the input buffer now contains: \nhello world .

Think of it like:
Buffer:
↓
[\n][h][e][l][l][o]..

Now:
getline(cin, s); starts reading from the buffer.
But the first thing it sees is: \n
and getline() stops when it encounters a newline.

So it reads: s = ""; When getline() encounters \n, it does not move to the next line and continue reading. Instead, it treats \n as the end of the current line and stops.
Think about this input:

A
hello world
                              ↓
Internally, it's stored as: A\nhello world\n . so when c++ reads this then it understands that ohh! I got to the end of line, so it will stop.

Suppose the buffer contains : \nhello world\n and you run: getline(cin, s);

It's like asking:
"Read characters until you see a newline."

But the very first character is already a newline.
So it reads: nothing and stores: s = "";
Therefore cin.ignore() is used to remove the leftover \n before calling getline().

| Function          | Variable Type | Reads                          |
| ----------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `cin >> c`        | `char`        | One character                  |
| `cin >> s`        | `string`      | One word                       |
| `getline(cin, s)` | `string`      | Entire line (including spaces) |

